src/backend/storage/lmgr/README

Locking Overview
================

PostgreSQL's four kinds of lock -- spinlocks, LWLocks, regular
("heavyweight") locks and SIReadLocks -- are gone from digres, with the
lock manager, the deadlock detector and the predicate lock manager. What
locks now (dg/SHMEM.md section 3):

* sm's locks.  A user's table, its rows and its schema are locked by sm1
(../sm/LOCK.md, sm1/SM1_DESIGN.md): a table-oid lock taken as a statement
opens the table, held to the transaction's end, and the row locks of its
reads and writes.  sm1 queues the waiters, finds deadlocks among them, and
gives serializable isolation; nothing of digres's takes part.

* lmgr.c's entry points.  What PostgreSQL's code asks of the lock manager
comes here.  A lock on a relation of sm's takes sm's table-oid lock
(smstore_lock_relation()); a catalog's takes nothing, as its rows are
compiled in and never change.  Each lock request then takes the
invalidations waiting (AcceptInvalidationMessages()).  What would have
waited for another transaction's lock waits for nothing.

* Advisory locks.  pg_advisory_lock() and the rest are in a table of
digres's own (advisory_lock.c): they guard nothing stored, so they are not
sm's.  It finds a deadlock among advisory waits only.

* Mutexes.  A short critical section is a std::mutex or std::shared_mutex
held by held_lock_t (storage/held_lock.h), which holds interrupts off
while it is held, and lets go as an error unwinds it.  None is held across
a wait.

* Waits.  A wait that must be cut short by a cancel, a timeout or a
shutdown is sm_wait() (sm_sys.h, ../sm/sm0/INTERRUPT.md), then
CHECK_FOR_INTERRUPTS().

No spinlock is taken (s_lock.c).  An atomic is a std::atomic, its memory
order explicit.
